This paper explores the possible value range and energy of the Seidel matrix for chain graphs, including a specific example that illustrates its maximum-possible value. Additionally, two criteria are established to determine the minimum Seidel energy for a particular graph class (chain graphs with no chords) and, as a consequence, an answer is given to a question posed in a previous study (Bipartite chain graphs and Seidel switching, Linear Algebra Appl., 2019). Specifically, the minimum Seidel energy is found to have a linear relationship with the number of edges in this specific graph class. It is also proven that, under certain conditions, the Seidel energy can be minimized by converting a chain graph into a cycle graph through chord additions. Lastly, two algorithms are devised to calculate the minimum Seidel energy and optimal chord addition strategy. Ultimately, a research area in graph theory known as "Seidel energy" is further developed and broadened by these findings, revealing additional methodologies and insights to facilitate future research. Keywords: Seidel energy, Seidel matrix, chain graphs, minimum Seidel energy, optimal chord addition. Mathematics Subject Classification: 15A69, 05C12. Size: 
